WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our next guest is
a voting-rights champion,

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.604
best-selling author,
former state representative,

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.939
and current candidate
for governor of Georgia.

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:09.808
Her children's book
"Stacey's Extraordinary Words"

00:00:09.808 --> 00:00:11.043
is on sale now.

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:13.045
Please welcome back to the show
Stacey Abrams.

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:14.547
How are you, Stacey?

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.548
-I am quite well.
How are you?

00:00:16.548 --> 00:00:18.218
-I'm doing fantastic.

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:21.221
I feel as though you had
a better week than I did.

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.056
For one, I'm at home with COVID,

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:26.059
but also, I am not
a Georgia Bulldogs fan.

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:28.394
And you were tweeting
about the game.

00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:29.795
You were watching it
with your father.

00:00:29.795 --> 00:00:31.897
Who was more excited,
you or him?

00:00:31.897 --> 00:00:33.232
-My dad.

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.668
There is no way to beat
a high school football star

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.138
when it comes to excitement
about a college football game.

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:40.473
I don't even bother trying.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:43.643
-Was it a fun father-daughter
evening to watch it together?

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.144
-It was delightful.

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:49.815
So, he got his start by becoming
famous on Instagram.

00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:51.751
He did not really understand
what that meant

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:55.488
until my 15-year-old niece
pointed out that 100,000 people

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:58.958
had liked his post and that
someone called him adorable,

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.928
which just made the halftime
for him.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.596
-[ Laughs ]
-And then we won.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:06.232
So, and my father grew up
in Mississippi,

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:09.702
so he grew up with a deep
animosity towards Bama.

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.204
So, you know, it was
a full-circle game for him.

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:15.241
-That's fantastic.
Congratulations to both of you.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.843
So, I want to start
by talking about the fact

00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:20.847
that speeches this week
from President Biden

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:23.015
and Vice President Harris
in Atlanta

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:27.020
about the importance of passing
voting-rights legislation --

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.521
you were absent.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:30.022
You said it was
due to scheduling.

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:31.691
But there were other
voting-rights groups

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:34.493
who boycotted the event
due to the slow progress

00:01:34.493 --> 00:01:35.862
on voting rights.

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:39.031
How do you feel about people
sort of using your absence

00:01:39.031 --> 00:01:42.367
as maybe a reflection on
the lack of Biden's popularity

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:44.336
in Georgia right now?

00:01:44.336 --> 00:01:47.740
-I think we are missing
the point, which is that we have

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.410
a president who understands
the attack on our democracy

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:56.316
that we talked about last week
when we remembered January 6th.

00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:58.317
But more importantly,
he's looking to a future

00:01:58.317 --> 00:02:01.654
where we should all be able
to participate in our elections.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:03.490
And even the groups
that were dispirited

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.158
have become re-engaged
and re-energized,

00:02:06.158 --> 00:02:08.828
and that's because
the president is doing his job.

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.898
He is a champion for voting
rights, and I'm proud to be

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.399
united with him in that fight.

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:15.568
-You talked a great deal
about voter suppression.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:18.737
Now you're running in a year
where I wouldn't say

00:02:18.737 --> 00:02:22.675
most pundits are bullish about
the chances for Democrats.

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:27.346
How do you feel Georgia is tied
to the sort of national polling,

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:29.782
right now, that is maybe not
looking particularly good

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.817
for your party?

00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:34.721
-Polling reflects
what people are feeling.

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:38.357
But running for office is about
telling people what's possible.

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:40.192
And I'm running on possibility.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:42.862
I know it is possible for us
to get healthcare for Georgians.

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.999
I know it's possible for us
to fully fund education.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.834
I know it's possible
for us to acknowledge

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.803
the pain people are feeling
economically,

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:53.339
but build a stronger
economic future.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.174
And that's what
I'm going to focus on.

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.577
And I think that's what we all
have to talk about.

00:02:57.577 --> 00:03:01.080
We cannot ignore the real pain
that people feel today.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.516
But part of the responsibility
of leadership

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:05.685
is to set a vision
for what's to come.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.953
And that's what
I'm going to run on.

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:10.289
-This looked, for a long time,
like it would be a rematch

00:03:10.289 --> 00:03:11.857
between you
and Governor Brian Kemp.

00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:15.328
And yet there is a Donald Trump
candidate, David Perdue,

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.196
who is primarying Kemp.

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:18.864
Is that something
you focus on at all?

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:23.402
Do you enjoy the fact that
there are two candidates

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:24.937
who are going to
eventually face off,

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:26.439
one of them is going to
face off with you,

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:29.341
sort of slinging mud
at one another right now?

00:03:29.341 --> 00:03:32.278
-There is a proverb that
I have completely appropriated

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:33.980
and twisted a little bit,
and it says,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.148
"When elephants fight,
stay off the grass."

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:37.650
I'm focusing on Georgia.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.952
I will let them do
what they want.

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:43.289
My job is to fight for
the future of Georgians.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.426
-A lot of focus has been
on Senator Joe Manchin

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:48.428
from West Virginia
and the power he sort of holds

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.129
over the Democratic Caucus
right now.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:52.564
Do you feel like that has
sort of fogged over the fact

00:03:52.564 --> 00:03:55.701
that Georgia won these two
runoff elections,

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:58.471
and Democrats could be
in a far worse place right now

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:01.240
than just having Joe Manchin
as a tipping point?

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.375
-I think we have to remember
that America would be

00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:07.279
in a far worse place but for
Senators Ossoff and Warnock;

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:10.482
that because of them, we have
seen child poverty cut in half;

00:04:10.482 --> 00:04:14.220
because of their two votes
added to the litany,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.155
we have seen
the American Rescue Plan;

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.489
we've seen
the infrastructure bill;

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:20.159
we are seeing progress
being made on so many levels.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.661
And a lot of Republican
governors are getting to spend

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:25.764
money that Democrats have
delivered into their states

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:27.499
to make certain that Americans,

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:29.335
regardless of who
they voted for,

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.837
get the services they desire
and the services they need.

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:34.941
And that's why I'm so proud that
I got to be part of making sure

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:36.809
that Jon Ossoff
and Raphael Warnock

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:38.945
are in the U.S. Senate.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.980
-We have talked, every time
you've been on the show,

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.149
about the fact -- about
how impressed I am

00:04:44.149 --> 00:04:46.151
that you still manage
to be an author.

00:04:46.151 --> 00:04:48.654
Not just a reader, not just
a lover of television,

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:52.325
but an accomplished author,
across many different genres --

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:55.661
political thrillers,
suspense, romance.

00:04:55.661 --> 00:04:57.330
And now you've written
a children's book.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.466
How is that different than
the other experiences?

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.135
-Children are hard, man.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:04.803
It is hard to write for kids.
[ Chuckles ]

00:05:04.803 --> 00:05:08.741
You've got to write a book
that they will sit still for.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:11.343
You've got to write a book that
their parents won't despise you

00:05:11.343 --> 00:05:14.012
for having to read over and over
again if they like it.

00:05:14.012 --> 00:05:17.216
And you have to take this
complicated story in your head

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:20.752
and not make it simple, but
distill it down so that it fits

00:05:20.752 --> 00:05:24.724
into a much shorter space
than a 100,000-word thriller.

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:28.561
And all in -- and make sure
that the art is amazing,

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:30.062
which luckily
I had nothing to do with,

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:33.166
because Kitt Thomas is
a fantastic artist.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.167
-I know you're very close
with nieces and nephews.

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.837
Did you use them as
a sounding board for this book?

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:40.072
And what was the sort of
feedback they gave you?

00:05:40.072 --> 00:05:41.907
-I did. And they were --

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:45.110
They are harsh critics,
but I satisfied them all

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.413
and got to a place where

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.815
I could show myself
at our holiday dinner

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:53.119
and they were pleased
to show their new books

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:56.655
to their friends,
so I passed the test.

00:05:56.655 --> 00:05:59.491
-And now, when you're on
the campaign trail,

00:05:59.491 --> 00:06:01.060
when you're traveling
around your state,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.561
do you still find time
to read?

00:06:02.561 --> 00:06:04.563
And what sort of things
do you like to read

00:06:04.563 --> 00:06:08.067
when you have sort of maybe
bigger, heavier topics

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.402
on your mind 24/7?

00:06:10.402 --> 00:06:12.905
-I read everything.
And yes, I read every day.

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:15.074
Right now, I am finishing up

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:17.209
"Harlem Shuffle"
by Colson Whitehead.

00:06:17.209 --> 00:06:20.078
I have a romance novel
by Nora Roberts

00:06:20.078 --> 00:06:21.747
that just makes me happy.

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:24.750
And that's going to be
the reading I do this weekend.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:28.320
And then I will find something
very heavy and weighty to read

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.991
because, well, why not ruin,
you know, my tomorrow?

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:34.493
-So, I think a lot of people,

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:37.897
maybe not just the voting-rights
groups that boycotted

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:40.833
the speeches by the president
and vice president this week,

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:42.668
are frustrated
by the lack of progress

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:45.971
and don't feel particularly
optimistic about

00:06:45.971 --> 00:06:49.675
any sort of voting-rights bills
getting through Congress.

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:52.278
You are somebody
who's in this every day.

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:55.781
Is there a reason for anyone
to feel optimistic right now?

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:57.216
-Absolutely.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:07:00.419
I live in Georgia, the heart
of the civil rights movement.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:01.653
But, more importantly,

00:07:01.653 --> 00:07:03.689
I'm the daughter of Robert
and Carolyn Abrams,

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:07.192
who fought for civil rights,
they fought for voting rights.

00:07:07.192 --> 00:07:09.762
My parents lived through,
you know,

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:12.297
the first 20 years
of their lives, basically,

00:07:12.297 --> 00:07:14.300
without full citizenship.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.269
I think we can wait another
few weeks and few months

00:07:17.269 --> 00:07:18.604
to get to where we need to be,

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.773
because, ultimately,
it's the goal.

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:23.075
And one thing I talk about in
"Stacey's Extraordinary Words"

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:26.078
is perseverance,
that we may not get what we want

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:28.380
in the moment we want it,
but our responsibility is

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:29.882
to never give up hope

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:32.050
and to never give up
the work we have to do.

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:33.385
The work is not done yet.

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:35.554
The work remains,
and we can do it.

00:07:35.554 --> 00:07:37.556
And that's what we saw
from the president this week,

00:07:37.556 --> 00:07:39.258
that's what we are going to
continue to see

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:40.826
in the U.S. Senate,

00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:42.528
and that's what
I'm going to continue to do

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:44.596
because I believe
in perseverance.

00:07:44.596 --> 00:07:46.932
And I can spell it, too.

00:07:46.932 --> 00:07:50.936
-Now, your book is about --
you mentioned spelling.

00:07:50.936 --> 00:07:53.438
Your book is about a girl
who enters a spelling bee.

00:07:53.438 --> 00:07:56.775
This was something -- words
and their correct spellings

00:07:56.775 --> 00:07:59.611
was something that you had
an early passion for, correct?

00:07:59.611 --> 00:08:01.680
-I did, indeed.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:05.718
-How would you go about --
Yeah, I'm sorry. Go ahead.

00:08:05.718 --> 00:08:08.954
-Oh, no, I was going to confess
my poor spelling of a word,

00:08:08.954 --> 00:08:10.289
but go ahead.

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:13.091
We can live that ignominy later.

00:08:13.091 --> 00:08:15.961
-Well, did you actually try
to learn how to spell words

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:18.297
as a kid, and how would you
go about it?

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:20.132
-I actually --
I didn't keep a notebook

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:21.466
of extraordinary words,

00:08:21.466 --> 00:08:23.302
but I did love words.

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:26.305
And my mom was a research
librarian when I was growing up,

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:27.807
so when she said,
"Go look it up,"

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:29.808
there really was no excuse.

00:08:29.808 --> 00:08:31.811
And after a while,
I would look up a word

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:33.812
and then read the words above it
and below it.

00:08:33.812 --> 00:08:35.481
Eventually,
I read the dictionary,

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:37.983
which, again, would not
recommend as bedtime reading,

00:08:37.983 --> 00:08:39.352
but was fun for me.

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:41.253
And I kept a list
of words that I liked.

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:44.590
And so spelling words
became a fun thing.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.326
-So, does that mean
that when you get a manuscript

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.562
to your editors,
be it for a children's book

00:08:49.562 --> 00:08:52.231
or for a suspense thriller,

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:55.567
is it hard for them to find
a misspelled word, do you think?

00:08:55.567 --> 00:08:57.736
-I do not misspell words, no.

00:08:57.736 --> 00:09:01.407
-Okay, gotcha.
What is your worst misspelling?

00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:04.410
-So, my very first major
misspelling was "chocolate."

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:05.744
-Okay.
-I am Southern.

00:09:05.744 --> 00:09:08.414
I had never heard it
pronounced chock-o-late,

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:10.882
so I did not know
there was an extra "O."

00:09:10.882 --> 00:09:14.119
It was devastating to me
to learn it onstage

00:09:14.119 --> 00:09:17.456
that there was a second "O"
in "chocolate."

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:19.458
So, that one sticks with me.
-Wait, onstage?

00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:22.461
Do you mean -- so you misspelled
this in a bee?

00:09:22.461 --> 00:09:23.962
-Oh. Oh, yeah.

00:09:23.962 --> 00:09:26.298
My very first spelling bee,
misspelled the word "chocolate."

00:09:26.298 --> 00:09:29.301
Yeah, it was devastating
on multiple levels.

00:09:29.301 --> 00:09:31.803
And as you can tell,
I've gotten over it.

00:09:31.803 --> 00:09:34.907
-Yeah, no, it's great that
it's not following you around

00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:36.442
like a cloud
all these years later.

00:09:36.442 --> 00:09:38.010
-No.
-[ Laughs ]

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.078
Hey, thank you so much
for joining us.

00:09:40.078 --> 00:09:41.413
Congrats on the book.

00:09:41.413 --> 00:09:42.915
Congrats on Georgia football.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.417
And we do hope
to see you again soon.

00:09:45.417 --> 00:09:47.819
-Thanks, Seth, so much.
It's always a pleasure.

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:49.488
-Stacey Abrams, everybody.

00:09:49.488 --> 00:09:51.790
"Stacey's Extraordinary Words"
is on sale now.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:53.392
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

